{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_UemrhqLl2O9HJju6mU8j72Th","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# spec-lint Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Spec File Discovery\n     7→The system SHALL discover all spec files in `openspec/specs/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/`.\n     8→\n     9→#### Scenario: Discover specs in standard location\n    10→- **WHEN** `se3 lint` is run\n    11→- **THEN** it finds all `spec.md` files under `openspec/specs/*/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/*/`\n    12→\n    13→### Requirement: Required Field Validation\n    14→The system SHALL validate that each spec file contains required fields.\n    15→\n    16→Required fields:\n    17→- `# <name> Specification` - Title header\n    18→- `## Purpose` - Purpose section explaining what this spec governs\n    19→- `## Requirements` - Requirements section with at least one requirement\n    20→- Each requirement MUST have scenarios (WHEN/THEN) or be marked as `**SHOULD**`/`**MAY**`\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Spec with missing Purpose\n    23→- **WHEN** a spec file lacks a Purpose section\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports an error with file path and line number\n    25→\n    26→#### Scenario: Spec with untestable requirement\n    27→- **WHEN** a requirement uses SHALL but has no WHEN/THEN scenario\n    28→- **THEN** `se3 lint` warns that the requirement may not be verifiable\n    29→\n    30→### Requirement: Scenario Format Validation\n    31→The system SHALL validate WHEN/THEN scenario format.\n    32→\n    33→Valid format:\n    34→```\n    35→#### Scenario: <name>\n    36→- **WHEN** <condition>\n    37→- **THEN** <expected outcome>\n    38→```\n    39→\n    40→#### Scenario: Malformed scenario\n    41→- **WHEN** a scenario is missing THEN clause\n    42→- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports the error with context\n    43→\n    44→### Requirement: Exit Codes\n    45→The system SHALL return appropriate exit codes.\n    46→\n    47→Exit codes:\n    48→- `0` - All specs valid\n    49→- `1` - One or more errors found\n    50→- `2` - Configuration or runtime error\n    51→\n    52→#### Scenario: CI integration\n    53→- **WHEN** `se3 lint` runs in CI pipeline\n    54→- **THEN** exit code 1 causes pipeline failure on spec violations\n    55→\n    56→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5df75db2-cfe8-41c7-992a-137f0218f267","uuid":"4f99979b-8775-4fab-9f0e-35aeb9e24021","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/.worktrees/spec-purpose-updates/openspec/specs/spec-lint/spec.md","content":"# spec-lint Specification\n\n## Purpose\nTBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Spec File Discovery\nThe system SHALL discover all spec files in `openspec/specs/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/`.\n\n#### Scenario: Discover specs in standard location\n- **WHEN** `se3 lint` is run\n- **THEN** it finds all `spec.md` files under `openspec/specs/*/` and `openspec/changes/*/specs/*/`\n\n### Requirement: Required Field Validation\nThe system SHALL validate that each spec file contains required fields.\n\nRequired fields:\n- `# <name> Specification` - Title header\n- `## Purpose` - Purpose section explaining what this spec governs\n- `## Requirements` - Requirements section with at least one requirement\n- Each requirement MUST have scenarios (WHEN/THEN) or be marked as `**SHOULD**`/`**MAY**`\n\n#### Scenario: Spec with missing Purpose\n- **WHEN** a spec file lacks a Purpose section\n- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports an error with file path and line number\n\n#### Scenario: Spec with untestable requirement\n- **WHEN** a requirement uses SHALL but has no WHEN/THEN scenario\n- **THEN** `se3 lint` warns that the requirement may not be verifiable\n\n### Requirement: Scenario Format Validation\nThe system SHALL validate WHEN/THEN scenario format.\n\nValid format:\n```\n#### Scenario: <name>\n- **WHEN** <condition>\n- **THEN** <expected outcome>\n```\n\n#### Scenario: Malformed scenario\n- **WHEN** a scenario is missing THEN clause\n- **THEN** `se3 lint` reports the error with context\n\n### Requirement: Exit Codes\nThe system SHALL return appropriate exit codes.\n\nExit codes:\n- `0` - All specs valid\n- `1` - One or more errors found\n- `2` - Configuration or runtime error\n\n#### Scenario: CI integration\n- **WHEN** `se3 lint` runs in CI pipeline\n- **THEN** exit code 1 causes pipeline failure on spec violations\n\n","numLines":56,"startLine":1,"totalLines":56}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_4dsQuCeXUlclf40O5SGhRDA7","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# requirement-intake Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change requirement-capture. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Three-Source Requirement Intake\n     7→The system SHALL accept new requirements from three distinct sources and route them through a unified `openspec change` creation process.\n     8→\n     9→**Source 1: Autonomous Discovery (Agent-Initiated)**\n    10→- Trigger: Agent identifies missing capability during implementation\n    11→- Flow: Agent creates new openspec change directly → proposal captures the discovered requirement\n    12→- Marker: `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` in proposal\n    13→\n    14→**Source 2: Human-MCP Call (Requested)**\n    15→- Trigger: Agent issues human call asking for requirements input\n    16→- Flow: Human responds → Agent parses response → Creates openspec change\n    17→- Marker: `[Source: human-mcp]` in proposal\n    18→\n    19→**Source 3: Human-Initiated (External)**\n    20→- Trigger: Human provides new requirement through any interaction (at natural conversation boundary or during active session)\n    21→- Flow: Agent recognizes human-initiated input → Creates new openspec change\n    22→- Marker: `[Source: human-initiated]` in proposal\n    23→- Note: \"Human-initiated\" is about **who originated the requirement** (human, not agent), not **how it was delivered** (interrupt or natural turn). All human-proposed requirements go through this path.\n    24→\n    25→#### Scenario: Autonomous discovery during implementation\n    26→- **WHEN** an agent realizes a needed feature wasn't in the original spec during coding\n    27→- **THEN** the agent creates a new openspec change with `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` marker\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Human provides requirements via MCP response\n    30→- **WHEN** a human fills in the Response section of a requirements-request call\n    31→- **THEN** the agent parses it and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-mcp]` marker\n    32→\n    33→#### Scenario: Human provides requirement through any interaction\n    34→- **WHEN** a human provides a new requirement (whether at natural conversation boundary or by interrupting active session)\n    35→- **THEN** the agent recognizes this as human-initiated input and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-initiated]` marker\n    36→- **NOTE** The distinction between \"interrupt\" and \"natural turn\" is not functionally significant; both are human-initiated requirements\n    37→\n    38→### Requirement: Unified Change Creation\n    39→All three intake sources SHALL result in the same downstream process: an openspec change with proposal → specs → implementation.\n    40→\n    41→#### Scenario: Consistent processing regardless of source\n    42→- **WHEN** any of the three sources triggers\n    43→- **THEN** the resulting openspec change follows the same SDD workflow (proposal → specs → design → tasks → code → verify → archive)\n    44→\n    45→### Requirement: Interruption Context Preservation\n    46→When Source 3 (human interrupt) occurs, the system SHALL preserve context of interrupted work.\n    47→\n    48→**Context preservation options:**\n    49→1. **Stack-based**: Push current change to stack, create new interrupt change, pop on completion\n    50→2. **Link-based**: New change references \"interrupted from: <change-id>\"\n    51→3. **Checkpoint-based**: Mark checkpoint in current change, resume from there after interrupt\n    52→\n    53→#### Scenario: Resume after interrupt\n    54→- **WHEN** a human-interrupt change completes\n    55→- **THEN** agent asks: \"Resume previous work on `<change-id>`? [Y/n]\"\n    56→\n    57→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5df75db2-cfe8-41c7-992a-137f0218f267","uuid":"8fc4484f-2b17-4488-b901-be5d6eaa25d4","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/.worktrees/spec-purpose-updates/openspec/specs/requirement-intake/spec.md","content":"# requirement-intake Specification\n\n## Purpose\nTBD - created by archiving change requirement-capture. Update Purpose after archive.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Three-Source Requirement Intake\nThe system SHALL accept new requirements from three distinct sources and route them through a unified `openspec change` creation process.\n\n**Source 1: Autonomous Discovery (Agent-Initiated)**\n- Trigger: Agent identifies missing capability during implementation\n- Flow: Agent creates new openspec change directly → proposal captures the discovered requirement\n- Marker: `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` in proposal\n\n**Source 2: Human-MCP Call (Requested)**\n- Trigger: Agent issues human call asking for requirements input\n- Flow: Human responds → Agent parses response → Creates openspec change\n- Marker: `[Source: human-mcp]` in proposal\n\n**Source 3: Human-Initiated (External)**\n- Trigger: Human provides new requirement through any interaction (at natural conversation boundary or during active session)\n- Flow: Agent recognizes human-initiated input → Creates new openspec change\n- Marker: `[Source: human-initiated]` in proposal\n- Note: \"Human-initiated\" is about **who originated the requirement** (human, not agent), not **how it was delivered** (interrupt or natural turn). All human-proposed requirements go through this path.\n\n#### Scenario: Autonomous discovery during implementation\n- **WHEN** an agent realizes a needed feature wasn't in the original spec during coding\n- **THEN** the agent creates a new openspec change with `[Source: autonomous-discovery]` marker\n\n#### Scenario: Human provides requirements via MCP response\n- **WHEN** a human fills in the Response section of a requirements-request call\n- **THEN** the agent parses it and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-mcp]` marker\n\n#### Scenario: Human provides requirement through any interaction\n- **WHEN** a human provides a new requirement (whether at natural conversation boundary or by interrupting active session)\n- **THEN** the agent recognizes this as human-initiated input and creates an openspec change with `[Source: human-initiated]` marker\n- **NOTE** The distinction between \"interrupt\" and \"natural turn\" is not functionally significant; both are human-initiated requirements\n\n### Requirement: Unified Change Creation\nAll three intake sources SHALL result in the same downstream process: an openspec change with proposal → specs → implementation.\n\n#### Scenario: Consistent processing regardless of source\n- **WHEN** any of the three sources triggers\n- **THEN** the resulting openspec change follows the same SDD workflow (proposal → specs → design → tasks → code → verify → archive)\n\n### Requirement: Interruption Context Preservation\nWhen Source 3 (human interrupt) occurs, the system SHALL preserve context of interrupted work.\n\n**Context preservation options:**\n1. **Stack-based**: Push current change to stack, create new interrupt change, pop on completion\n2. **Link-based**: New change references \"interrupted from: <change-id>\"\n3. **Checkpoint-based**: Mark checkpoint in current change, resume from there after interrupt\n\n#### Scenario: Resume after interrupt\n- **WHEN** a human-interrupt change completes\n- **THEN** agent asks: \"Resume previous work on `<change-id>`? [Y/n]\"\n\n","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"tool_QldynqdSdyhGqZzJz6jJ69hS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# change-verifier Specification\n     2→\n     3→## Purpose\n     4→TBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n     5→## Requirements\n     6→### Requirement: Spec Scenario Extraction\n     7→The system SHALL extract all WHEN/THEN scenarios from a change's specs.\n     8→\n     9→#### Scenario: Extract scenarios from change\n    10→- **WHEN** `se3 verify --change <name>` is run\n    11→- **THEN** it parses all spec files in `openspec/changes/<name>/specs/`\n    12→- **AND** extracts a list of scenario IDs (format: `<spec-name>/<scenario-name>`)\n    13→\n    14→### Requirement: Implementation Detection\n    15→The system SHALL detect evidence of scenario implementation.\n    16→\n    17→Detection methods (in order of confidence):\n    18→1. Test file with `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"<id>\")` decorator\n    19→2. Code comment `# Verify: <scenario-id>`\n    20→3. Spec archive with \"implemented\" marker\n    21→\n    22→#### Scenario: Find test marker\n    23→- **WHEN** a test file contains `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"session-protocol/first-time-bootstrap\")`\n    24→- **THEN** `se3 verify` marks that scenario as covered\n    25→\n    26→### Requirement: Coverage Report\n    27→The system SHALL generate a coverage report showing implemented vs missing scenarios.\n    28→\n    29→#### Scenario: Incomplete implementation\n    30→- **WHEN** some scenarios have no detected implementation\n    31→- **THEN** `se3 verify` lists uncovered scenarios with file paths\n    32→- **AND** exits with code 1\n    33→\n    34→#### Scenario: Complete implementation\n    35→- **WHEN** all scenarios have detected implementation\n    36→- **THEN** `se3 verify` reports success\n    37→- **AND** exits with code 0\n    38→\n    39→### Requirement: Skip Annotation\n    40→The system SHALL support explicitly skipping scenarios.\n    41→\n    42→#### Scenario: Skip with reason\n    43→- **WHEN** a spec contains `<!-- verify-skip: reason -->` before a scenario\n    44→- **THEN** that scenario is excluded from coverage requirements\n    45→\n    46→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5df75db2-cfe8-41c7-992a-137f0218f267","uuid":"3244b275-37fc-47ef-917d-8e4ab35645e8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/data/cre/workspace/se3.0/.worktrees/spec-purpose-updates/openspec/specs/change-verifier/spec.md","content":"# change-verifier Specification\n\n## Purpose\nTBD - created by archiving change toolize-se3. Update Purpose after archive.\n## Requirements\n### Requirement: Spec Scenario Extraction\nThe system SHALL extract all WHEN/THEN scenarios from a change's specs.\n\n#### Scenario: Extract scenarios from change\n- **WHEN** `se3 verify --change <name>` is run\n- **THEN** it parses all spec files in `openspec/changes/<name>/specs/`\n- **AND** extracts a list of scenario IDs (format: `<spec-name>/<scenario-name>`)\n\n### Requirement: Implementation Detection\nThe system SHALL detect evidence of scenario implementation.\n\nDetection methods (in order of confidence):\n1. Test file with `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"<id>\")` decorator\n2. Code comment `# Verify: <scenario-id>`\n3. Spec archive with \"implemented\" marker\n\n#### Scenario: Find test marker\n- **WHEN** a test file contains `@pytest.mark.scenario(\"session-protocol/first-time-bootstrap\")`\n- **THEN** `se3 verify` marks that scenario as covered\n\n### Requirement: Coverage Report\nThe system SHALL generate a coverage report showing implemented vs missing scenarios.\n\n#### Scenario: Incomplete implementation\n- **WHEN** some scenarios have no detected implementation\n- **THEN** `se3 verify` lists uncovered scenarios with file paths\n- **AND** exits with code 1\n\n#### Scenario: Complete implementation\n- **WHEN** all scenarios have detected implementation\n- **THEN** `se3 verify` reports success\n- **AND** exits with code 0\n\n### Requirement: Skip Annotation\nThe system SHALL support explicitly skipping scenarios.\n\n#### Scenario: Skip with reason\n- **WHEN** a spec contains `<!-- verify-skip: reason -->` before a scenario\n- **THEN** that scenario is excluded from coverage requirements\n\n","numLines":46,"startLine":1,"totalLines":46}}}
